# SOLID Principles

Apply these when designing classes, modules, and components.

## S – Single Responsibility (SRP)
Each class/module should have one reason to change. One concern per unit.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD: UserService does auth + persistence + email
class UserService {
  login() {}
  saveToDb() {}
  sendWelcomeEmail() {}
}

// ✅ GOOD: Separate responsibilities
class AuthService { login() {} }
class UserRepository { save() {} }
class EmailService { sendWelcome() {} }
```

## O – Open/Closed (OCP)
Open for extension, closed for modification. Prefer composition and strategy over editing existing code.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD: Adding a new format requires changing the function
function exportData(format: string) {
  if (format === 'json') return toJson();
  if (format === 'csv') return toCsv();
}

// ✅ GOOD: New exporters without changing existing code
interface Exporter { export(data: Data): string; }
class JsonExporter implements Exporter { export(data) { return toJson(data); } }
class CsvExporter implements Exporter { export(data) { return toCsv(data); } }
```

## L – Liskov Substitution (LSP)
Subtypes must be substitutable for their base type. Don’t break contracts or throw in cases the base type doesn’t.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD: Subclass narrows contract (throws where base doesn't)
class Rectangle { setSize(w: number, h: number) { /* ... */ } }
class Square extends Rectangle { setSize(w: number, h: number) { if (w !== h) throw new Error(); /* ... */ } }

// ✅ GOOD: Prefer composition when behavior diverges so the contract stays clear
class Square {
  constructor(private side: number) {}
  setSize(s: number) {
    this.side = s;
  }
}
// Subclass only when fully substitutable; never strengthen preconditions or throw where base doesn't.
```

## I – Interface Segregation (ISP)
Prefer small, focused interfaces over one large interface. Callers should not depend on methods they don’t use.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD: One fat interface
interface Worker { work(); eat(); sleep(); }

// ✅ GOOD: Segregated interfaces
interface Workable { work(); }
interface Eatable { eat(); }
interface Sleepable { sleep(); }
```

## D – Dependency Inversion (DIP)
Depend on abstractions (interfaces, types), not concrete implementations. Inject dependencies.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD: Direct dependency on concrete class
class OrderService {
  private db = new PostgresDatabase();
}

// ✅ GOOD: Depend on abstraction, inject implementation
interface OrderRepository { save(order: Order): Promise<void>; }
class OrderService {
  constructor(private repo: OrderRepository) {}
}
```

When suggesting or refactoring code, favor SOLID: single responsibility, extension over modification, correct substitution, small interfaces, and abstraction-based dependencies.
